{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And if it is a white baheret.</b> But it is not white, but rather the appearance of the neg'a is deeper than the skin, as it is written: <b>and its hair has not turned white, and it is not deeper in appearance.</b> Rashi wrote: 'I do not know its meaning' — that is, since it is white, how is it possible that its appearance is not deep? And one may say <b>ועמק אין מראה</b> is like the case of a boil, as stated in the Tosefta: from where do we include that which is level with what is elevated? — meaning that the neg'a is level with the rest of the flesh. Scripture says: <b>ושפלה איננה</b>; here too it was necessary to write <b>ועמק אין מראה</b> מן העור, to include that which is level with what is elevated." "<b>And its hair has not turned white.</b> And its original hair — the letter heh does not take it out.'" "<b>And the kohen shall confine it.</b> And at the end of seven days the kohen inspects it to see if there has been spreading in it, for one who sees a thing frequently, and it grows little by little, does not notice its growth as much as one who sees it only occasionally."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {1 "Reading ה״א as 'the letter heh,' i.e., the definite article on שערה does not remove the law from applying to pre-existing hair."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:44.646035406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}